`@` reads stdin when the request needs input. Inside a quoted request, a bare `@` passes one stage’s output to the next.

```bash
echo @ | @ ot                       # yhwh
@ 'echo 木 @ pinyin @ uppercase'     # MÙ
```

In `nsh`, use `:paste` for multiline input and `ld.so COMMAND` to run Bash directly. See `man @`, `nsh --help`, or `LD --help` for more.
